---
// Sources and licences of the Analects Reader (src/pages/sources.astro).
import { GLOSS_CODES } from "../../packs/lzh/morph";
import { VARIANTS } from "../../packs/lzh/spelling";
import { DIFFERENCES, MISSING_MARKERS, REPEATED, SUBSTITUTES } from "./alignment";
import { KAIKKI, KYOTO_COMMIT, LEGGE, UNICODE_VERSION } from "./sources";

const treebank = `https://github.com/UniversalDependencies/UD_Classical_Chinese-Kyoto/tree/${KYOTO_COMMIT}`;
const shown = (chars: string) => chars || "nothing";
---
<h2>Classical Chinese</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Kyoto University Treebank for Classical Chinese, in Universal Dependencies</dt>
  <dd>
    The characters, words, lemmas, parts of speech, features and English glosses of every token come
    from <a href="https://github.com/UniversalDependencies/UD_Classical_Chinese-Kyoto">UD_Classical_Chinese-Kyoto</a>,
    Universal Dependencies 2.18, pinned to commit <a href={treebank}><code>{KYOTO_COMMIT}</code></a>: the work of
    Koichi Yasuoka, Christian Wittern and others at the Institute for Research in Humanities, Kyoto University. The
    Analects are its documents <code>KR1h0004_001</code> to <code>_020</code>, which it spreads over its training,
    development and test files; the reader merges them in book order. The treebank’s text is the Kanripo / CHANT
    edition of the Analects. Its lemmas, parts of speech and features were annotated by hand in the Kyoto scheme and
    converted to Universal Dependencies, with some manual corrections. The repository’s README metadata says “PD”
    but its <code>LICENSE.txt</code>
    says <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>; the reader follows the licence
    file, so its Chinese text and annotation, and the word data built from them, may be reused only under the same
    licence, with attribution.
  </dd>
  <dt>Words</dt>
  <dd>
    The text is the treebank’s, with its word division: a word of several characters
    (<i lang="lzh-Hant">君子</i>, <i lang="lzh-Hant">孔子</i>) is one clickable piece. The treebank lemmatizes in Kanripo’s forms;
    where the text prints another form of the same character, the reader shows the printed one as the lemma
    ({Object.entries(VARIANTS).map(([from, to], i) => <>{i ? ", " : ""}<span lang="lzh-Hant">{from} as {to}</span></>)}),
    and lexicon, dictionary and search treat the pair as one spelling. Unihan relates nine of these twelve pairs as
    variants; the build checks that it does not relate the other three.
  </dd>
  <dt>Parts of speech and glosses</dt>
  <dd>
    The treebank’s Universal Dependencies word classes (it has no adjectives: those are verbs) and its sparse
    features are kept and labelled in English, and the first line of the word panel is the treebank’s own English
    gloss of the word in this place. Its own, finer tags (“verb, action, motion”, “noun, person, role”) supply the
    class under the word class. Words with no English equivalent are glossed with codes in brackets, which the panel
    spells out:
    <details>
      <summary>The treebank’s gloss codes</summary>
      <ul>
        {Object.entries(GLOSS_CODES).map(([code, meaning]) => <li><code>{code}</code> {meaning}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </details>
    The treebank also gives every word its place in a dependency tree; this reader does not show it.
  </dd>
  <dt>Punctuation</dt>
  <dd>
    The treebank is unpunctuated. The reader prints the marks <span lang="lzh-Hant">、 。 ； ？</span> of
    Legge’s Chinese text (below): the characters of each book in the two texts are aligned, and a mark Legge prints after
    a character is printed after the word of the treebank that ends with the corresponding character. The marks are
    Legge’s reading of the sentences, not the treebank’s; a line ends after each <span lang="lzh-Hant">。</span>. The
    marks are never inside a word and never change the text, and search ignores them.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>English and chapters</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1: <i>Confucian Analects, the Great Learning and the Doctrine of the Mean</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1861; 2nd ed. 1893)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. The English, the chapter divisions (499, numbered as Legge numbers them) and the punctuation come
    from <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/4094">Project Gutenberg #4094</a>, which prints Legge’s Chinese with his English,
    fetched from <code>{LEGGE[0]}</code> and checked against SHA-256 <code>{LEGGE[1]}</code>. Project
    Gutenberg regenerates this file, so a change upstream stops the fetch until the new copy is checked. The English
    keeps Legge’s romanizations (<i>Hsio R</i>, <i>Chung-ni</i>) and his book headings, which this reader shows after
    each book’s first words; his notes are not included. The reader’s chapters are Legge’s: the treebank’s own paragraph
    divisions, which differ from his (book 10 has 27 paragraphs there and 18 chapters in Legge’s), are not used.
  </dd>
  <dt>Aligning Legge’s Chinese with the treebank’s</dt>
  <dd>
    Legge’s Chinese was typed in the Big5 encoding, which has no code for some rare characters: where Project Gutenberg
    describes one in parentheses, the build replaces it with the treebank’s character
    ({Object.values(SUBSTITUTES).map((char, i) => <>{i ? ", " : ""}<span lang="lzh-Hant">{char}</span></>)}). The characters of
    each book are then aligned one against the other. The build stops on any difference it has not been told about:
    <ul>
      <li>
        One-for-one substitutions of up to two characters are accepted as variants or other readings (<span lang="lzh-Hant">修</span>
        and <span lang="lzh-Hant">脩</span>, <span lang="lzh-Hant">惟</span> and <span lang="lzh-Hant">唯</span>) or
        as typographical slips of one text: Legge’s text prints <span lang="lzh-Hant">李</span> for <span lang="lzh-Hant">季</span> nineteen times, in
        the name of the Ji clan. The page always shows the treebank’s character.
      </li>
      <li>
        Fourteen other differences, a character that one text has and the other lacks (three in book 4), are each listed in the build with the characters before them:
        <details>
          <summary>Where the texts differ</summary>
          <ul>
            {DIFFERENCES.map((d) => <li>Book {d.book}, after <span lang="lzh-Hant">{d.before}</span>: the treebank has <span lang="lzh-Hant">{shown(d.kyoto)}</span>, Legge <span lang="lzh-Hant">{shown(d.legge)}</span></li>)}
          </ul>
        </details>
      </li>
      <li>
        Six chapters lack their number in Legge’s Chinese, though his English numbers them; each starts at the words
        <details>
          <summary>Chapters found by their first words</summary>
          <ul>
            {Object.entries(MISSING_MARKERS).map(([chapter, words]) => <li>{chapter}: <span lang="lzh-Hant">{words}</span></li>)}
          </ul>
        </details>
      </li>
      <li>
        Book 17 prints <span lang="lzh-Hant">{REPEATED[0]!.text}</span> twice in a row in the treebank (once in each
        of its two paragraphs); the second copy is left out of the alignment but stays in the text.
      </li>
    </ul>
    The word that Legge’s mark follows must end a treebank word; a mark inside a word would stop the build.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Dictionaries</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Unicode Unihan database {UNICODE_VERSION}</dt>
  <dd>
    <a href="https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html">Unihan</a>’s <code>kDefinition</code>, <code>kMandarin</code>
    and <code>kCantonese</code> give the one-line definition and the pinyin and Jyutping of a character, and its variant
    relations check the spelling pairs above. © Unicode, Inc., used under
    the <a href="https://www.unicode.org/copyright.html">Unicode Terms of Use</a>.
  </dd>
  <dt>Wiktionary</dt>
  <dd>
    The entries of the English Wiktionary for Chinese, as extracted
    by <a href="https://github.com/tatuylonen/wiktextract">Wiktextract</a> and published
    at <a href="https://kaikki.org/dictionary/Chinese/">kaikki.org</a>,
    licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> and the GNU Free Documentation License;
    © Wiktionary contributors. The reader takes each lemma’s definitions (literary senses first), its pinyin, its Middle
    Chinese (in Baxter’s transcription) and its Old Chinese reconstructions. The dump is regenerated about weekly
    and is not versioned: the build pins the snapshot with SHA-256 <code>{KAIKKI[1]}</code> (retrieved
    from <code>{KAIKKI[0]}</code>) and the fetch stops when kaikki.org publishes another, which then has to be checked.
  </dd>
  <dt>The treebank’s glosses</dt>
  <dd>
    Each lemma’s page lists the glosses the treebank gives it, for each word class, with the number of occurrences.
    The lexicon’s short gloss is the lemma’s pinyin and its most frequent gloss for the word class.
  </dd>
</dl>

<style>
  code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
